I built the issue desk in plain TypeScript under `/tmp/claude-0/runs/r15/desk9-vanilla-opus-5/src/`: `main.ts` holds the tabs and the open-issue count, and there is one file per page (`issues.ts`, `help.ts`, `settings.ts`), plus shared helpers in `dom.ts` and `app.ts`. Every change shows on screen straight away and is undone if the server declines; answers that arrive late or out of order are ignored, and so is anything that comes back for a page that has been left. All 30 visible tests pass and `./check` is clean, using 2 of the 15 runs.

The brief left some cases open and I made calls on them. A decline that arrives after the user has left the issues page still puts the count back, and in-flight assigns, renames and closes are tracked across pages. A decline from an older action is not shown if a newer action of the same kind on the same issue has started since. Clicking a related hit fills the help page with the hits the related search already returned rather than searching again. A display name made only of spaces counts as empty.